# Docker rebuild after code changes

When you change code that runs **inside Docker containers**, you must rebuild images — source is not bind-mounted.

## Trigger paths (rebuild required)

- `backend/**` (market-api / ai-news image)
- `iros-terminal/**` (frontend image)
- `docker-compose.yml`, `backend/Dockerfile`, `iros-terminal/Dockerfile`
- `config/cloudflare/**` used by the cloudflared service (credentials prepare still runs at start)

## Command (always)

From repo root after finishing those edits:

```bat
rebuild-docker.bat
```

What it does: stop stack → `docker compose build --no-cache` → up with tunnel profile → `docker image prune` (delete old/dangling images) → health wait.

Faster iteration only when user asks: `rebuild-docker.bat --cached`

## Do not

- Assume `start-docker.bat --no-build` picks up code edits
- Leave dangling `<none>` images after a rebuild without pruning (the bat handles prune)
- Tell the user to rebuild manually without offering/running `rebuild-docker.bat` when you changed container code in-session

## Native vs Docker

- Native (`start-app.bat`): no image rebuild
- Docker desk: use `rebuild-docker.bat` after container code changes; use `start-docker.bat` only for start without rebuild
